  "body": {
    "id": "lem:stopped-moment-consistency",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "Along the retained even tail \\(n\\ge N_0\\), every treated- and control-arm empirical mean entering def:observable-envelope-inputs differs from its finite-population counterpart by \\(o_{\\Pr}(1)\\) at \\(T_n\\). Consequently, \\(\\widehat b_n-b_n\\to_{\\Pr}0\\) and \\(\\widehat u_n-u_n\\to_{\\Pr}0\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "tailIndex",
      "stoppedAllocation",
      "stopTime",
      "estimatedLoading",
      "projectionLoading",
      "estimatedEnvelope",
      "envelopeVariance",
      "covariates",
      "outcome0",
      "outcome1"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:regular-tail",
      "ass:uniform-boundedness",
      "ass:covariate-nondegeneracy",
      "thm:joint-stopped-functional-limit",
      "def:variance-envelope",
      "def:observable-envelope-inputs",
      "lem:regular-tail-wellposedness"
    ],
    "justification": "Adaptive first entry selects the terminal arms through the whole covariate path, so fixed-draw laws of large numbers cannot simply be conditioned on acceptance.",
    "gap": "Li and Ding establish armwise regression quantities under uniform rerandomization; the stopped nonuniform PSRR design has no published uniform path law.",
    "consumer": "The result makes all projection and variance inputs in the proposed interval computable from the realized experiment.",
    "proof_tex": "The joint functional-limit theorem implies \\(T_n/n=O_{\\Pr}(1)\\).  Fix \\(\\eta>0\\).  Choose \\(L<\\infty\\) such that \\(\\limsup_n\\Pr(T_n>nL)<\\eta\\).  Apply actual-path arm uniformity to the finite collection of bounded deterministic arrays\n\\[\n 1,\\quad x_{nij},\\quad x_{nij}x_{ni\\ell},\\quad Y_{ni}(z),\\quad\n x_{nij}Y_{ni}(z),\\quad Y_{ni}(z)^2,\n \\qquad z\\in\\{0,1\\},\\ 1\\le j,\\ell\\le k. \\tag{20}\n\\]\nUniform boundedness verifies the envelope condition.  On \\(\\{T_n\\le nL\\}\\), every treated- and control-arm raw empirical moment in (20), evaluated at \\(T_n\\), differs from its full-population counterpart by \\(o_{\\Pr}(1)\\).  The probability of the complement is at most \\(\\eta+o(1)\\); letting \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\) proves the same statement unconditionally.\n\nCentered covariances are polynomial functions of these raw moments, so the observed arm covariance and outcome--covariate vectors differ from \\(S_{xx,n}\\) and \\(S_{x,Y(z),n}\\) by \\(o_{\\Pr}(1)\\), with the immaterial divisor corrections also \\(o(1)\\).  Since \\(\\lambda_{\\min}(S_{xx,n})\\ge c_x/2\\), eigenvalue continuity implies that both observed arm Gram matrices are nonsingular with probability tending to one, and their inverses differ from \\(S_{xx,n}^{-1}\\) by \\(o_{\\Pr}(1)\\).  Thus the Moore--Penrose convention on the vanishing singular event has no first-order effect.\n\nThe same continuous algebra gives, row by row,\n\\[\n \\widehat c_{z,n}-S_{x,Y(z),n}=o_{\\Pr}(1),\\qquad\n \\widehat S_{e_z,n}^2-S_{e_ze_z,n}=o_{\\Pr}(1). \\tag{21}\n\\]\nThe inverse square roots of \\(S_{xx,n}\\) are uniformly bounded, so the definitions and (21) yield\n\\[\n \\widehat b_n-b_n\n =S_{xx,n}^{-1/2}\\{(\\widehat c_{1,n}-S_{x,Y(1),n})+(\\widehat c_{0,n}-S_{x,Y(0),n})\\}\n \\longrightarrow_{\\Pr}0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n \\widehat u_n-u_n\n =2(\\widehat S_{e_1,n}^2-S_{e_1e_1,n})+2(\\widehat S_{e_0,n}^2-S_{e_0e_0,n})\n \\longrightarrow_{\\Pr}0.\n\\]\nThis proves stopped-design consistency without conditioning the terminal allocation on uniform acceptance.",
